In dense payload configurations, the suppression of 19th harmonics through filtering can significantly reduce intermodulation products (IMD) and adjacent channel interference (ACI), which are major sources of power loss and degradation in the transponder. These distortions are generated due to the nonlinear behavior of high-power amplifiers (HPAs) when multiple carriers are amplified simultaneously.

For instance, in a scenario where multiple carriers are transmitted through an HPA, the 19th harmonics can interact with other signals to produce IMD, leading to increased ACI and ISI. This not only reduces the overall power efficiency but also lowers the signal-to-noise ratio (SNR), affecting the quality of the transmitted data.

Filtering out the 19th harmonics can mitigate these effects. For example, using a filter with a high-order Chebyshev or Butterworth response can effectively suppress these harmonics while maintaining a flat passband response. This approach can be particularly beneficial in systems employing advanced modulation schemes like 4+12 APSK, which are more sensitive to nonlinear distortions.

However, the effectiveness of this filtering depends on the specific modulation scheme. For instance, 4+12 APSK, with its concentrated outer ring amplitude, can benefit more from harmonic suppression compared to 16QAM, which has a more uniform amplitude distribution.

In summary, filtering out 19th harmonics can improve power efficiency by reducing IMD and ACI, but the design and implementation of the filter must be carefully considered to maximize its benefits in dense payload configurations.

