The primary challenge in compensating for pattern degradation in array-fed reflectors during element failures is the significant gain loss, especially at the edges of the illuminated domain. MVDR beamforming can mitigate some of this loss, but its effectiveness varies. For instance, Table II shows a slight reduction in gain after MVDR beamforming, with the conventional reflector suffering more severe losses.

MVDR beamforming can help, but it is not a panacea. It may not fully compensate for the loss, particularly in critical directions. Design considerations include adding border feed elements or using a curved feed array to reduce the gain roll-off. However, these solutions come with additional complexity and cost.

