 The primary MIMO requirements for high-speed aircraft communication aim to increase data rates and reduce interference. To achieve this, the number of transmit and receive antennas is increased for spatial diversity and spatial multiplexing. However, these requirements can conflict with aerodynamic constraints, which limit the size and complexity of the antennas.

The MIMO needs impact the array factor, spacing, and overall performance of the antenna. A larger number of antennas can lead to better performance, but the array factor becomes more complex, increasing the potential for interference. The spacing between antennas affects the spatial diversity and multiplexing gains, with closer spacing providing better performance but increasing the aerodynamic drag.

The trade-offs in terms of capacity and interference are crucial. A denser antenna array can provide higher capacity but may also increase the potential for interference. On the other hand, a sparse array may have lower capacity but less interference. Designers must balance these trade-offs to create an efficient and aerodynamically friendly MIMO antenna system for high-speed aircraft communication.

